1. [derived from Facts 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] The source presents orchestration as a pattern composed of dividing tasks into stages, assigning responsibilities to specialized agents, and coordinating them via an orchestration layer, and it says this pattern is central to Nuëyon.Compose.
2. [derived from Facts 12–18, 19–21] The source represents Compose's process as a staged workflow that separates what is discovered and researched (upstream stages) from how material is presented (final composition stage).
3. [derived from Facts 6, 7, 27, 28, 29, 36] The source links the orchestration/structured-application approach in Compose to possible reuse of orchestration and knowledge-layer capabilities across other product directions.

